# Summary
Introduced: restart validation now turns METADATA_STATE_DIGEST_UNVERIFIED into a persisted digest of the current materialized rows. This removes the previous fail-closed behavior for unverified large command streams and can admit corrupted/tampered metadata as clean.
For placement groups whose applied metadata command index exceeds the online digest limit, the stored state digest is set to the sentinel value 0. Before this commit, restart validation rejected that sentinel as unverified. The new code recomputes a digest from the current SQLite materialized metadata and stores it as the trusted digest without replaying the command log to prove that the rows are the result of the accepted command history. If the materialized metadata was corrupted or tampered with while the sentinel was present, restart can now make that corruption authoritative. Cross-replica agreement only catches differing replicas; it does not protect single-node configurations or identical corruption/tampering across replicas. Metadata includes security-relevant fields such as bucket/object ACL/public flags, ownership, encryption state, and object layout, so accepting bad metadata can affect confidentiality and integrity.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm large metadata command streams can persist the unverified digest sentinel 0 after the online digest limit.
- [x] Confirm restart validation handles sentinel 0 by digesting current materialized SQLite metadata and persisting that digest, rather than failing closed.
- [x] Demonstrate tampering of a security-relevant materialized metadata field while sentinel 0 is present.
- [x] Demonstrate a single-node restart succeeds and changes state_digest from 0 to a nonzero trusted digest over tampered rows.
- [x] Demonstrate a subsequent restart accepts the tampered metadata as clean/trusted.
## Report
Validated the finding as a logic/integrity vulnerability. Code review showed the relevant path: crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:1377-1385 returns METADATA_STATE_DIGEST_UNVERIFIED/0 once applied_log_index > 128; crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:820-824 recomputes metadata_state_digest() from the current materialized SQLite rows when it sees sentinel 0; crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:1154-1164 persists that recomputed digest into metadata_command_replica_state; crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:1272-1309 compares only the post-validation replica states. The bucket table includes security-relevant public_read/public_write/ACL fields at crates/storage/src/schema.rs:373-375.

Dynamic PoC: I injected a targeted unit test that opens a valid single-node local cluster, applies 129 metadata create-bucket commands so the stored digest becomes sentinel 0, directly tampers SQLite with `UPDATE buckets SET public_read = 1`, then reopens the cluster. The reopen succeeded, stored a nonzero digest over the tampered rows, and a second reopen accepted that digest as trusted. Command output: `before_restart: applied_log_index=129 state_digest=0`; `after_direct_sql_tamper: bucket=validation-poc-1-0 public_read=true`; `after_restart: applied_log_index=129 state_digest=7301272145618881068`; `second_restart: trusted_digest=7301272145618881068 public_read=true`; test result ok. This demonstrates corrupted/tampered materialized metadata can become authoritative in a single-node configuration.

Crash/valgrind/debugger attempts: direct cargo test reproduced the logic issue without a crash; valgrind was not installed; lldb was available and ran the PoC test non-interactively, exiting status 0 after printing the same acceptance evidence. Because this is not a memory-safety crash, successful non-crashing behavior is the vulnerability.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit 4f50981 (`Harden metadata digest validation`).

The fix removes `METADATA_STATE_DIGEST_UNVERIFIED` and no longer treats any persisted digest value as an invitation to trust current materialized SQLite rows on restart. Metadata command apply now maintains a durable per-table digest cache: before applying a command it verifies the command-owned tables that command can touch, then refreshes those table digests and persists the composed PG state digest as part of command state advancement. Restart validation still recomputes the full materialized metadata digest from SQLite rows and compares it to the persisted command replica state, so tampered materialized rows fail closed instead of becoming the new trusted baseline.

The canonical metadata digest was also tightened: each table range carries explicit table/filter/column/order-column identity, text values remain length-prefixed, and blob values are represented as byte length plus CRC64 of the blob contents. Object payload bytes are not included in the metadata command log, but committed descriptor rows include payload CRC64 fields and those rows are digest-covered.

Regression coverage includes a large-prefix reopen test that applies 129 metadata commands, tampers a bucket `public_read` row, and asserts `LocalClusterMap::open` rejects the store with `MetadataStateDigestMismatch`. Additional focused tests cover digest-covered command-owned segment/reclaim tables and the online affected-table verification path.

